tion>Environ Pollut. 2019 Jul;250:863-872</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">31085472</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Nucleic Acids Res. 2005 Jan 1;33(Database issue):D34-8</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15608212</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Nucleic Acids Res. 2007;35(21):7188-96</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">17947321</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Environ Microbiol. 2015 Sep;17(9):3219-32</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">25580993</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Environ Microbiol. 1999 Nov;65(11):4887-97</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">10543800</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Environ Microbiol. 2010 Apr;12(4):862-72</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20050875</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Microbiol Rev. 1996 Jun;60(2):439-71</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8801441</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Microbiol Biotechnol. 2011 Nov;92(4):845-54</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21667086</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Environ Microbiol Rep. 2009 Oct;1(5):293-306</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">23765882</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Microbiol Biotechnol. 2018 Sep;102(17):7365-7375</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29982927</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>PLoS One. 2012;7(6):e38600</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22737213</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Microb Ecol. 2018 Apr;75(3):761-770</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29022063</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Microbiol Biotechnol. 2019 Jan;103(1):1-8</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30315351</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Microbiol Biotechnol. 2015 Jul;99(14):5763-79</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26051673</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Appl Environ Microbiol. 1997 Sep;63(9):3367-73</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">9292986</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Mol Biol Evol. 2016 Jul;33(7):1870-4</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">27004904</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">33371270</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2021</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>01</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">2076-2607</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>8</Volume><Issue>12</Issue><PubDate><Year>2020</Year><Month>Dec</Month><Day>21</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Microorganisms</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Microorganisms</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Methane-Oxidizing Communities in Lichen-Dominated Forested Tundra Are Composed Exclusively of High-Affinity USC&#x3b1; Methanotrophs.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">2047</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3390/microorganisms8122047</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Upland soils of tundra function as a constant sink for atmospheric CH<sub>4</sub> but the identity of methane oxidizers in these soils remains poorly understood. Methane uptake rates of -0.4 to -0.6 mg CH<sub>4</sub>-C m<sup>-2</sup> day<sup>-1</sup> were determined by the static chamber method in a mildly acidic upland soil of the lichen-dominated forested tundra, North Siberia, Russia. The maximal CH<sub>4</sub> oxidation activity was localized in an organic surface soil layer underlying the lichen cover. Molecular identification of methanotrophic bacteria based on retrieval of the <i>pmoA</i> gene revealed Upland Soil Cluster Alpha (USC&#x3b1;) as the only detectable methanotroph group. Quantification of these <i>pmoA</i> gene fragments by means of specific qPCR assay detected ~10<sup>7</sup><i>pmoA</i> gene copies g<sup>-1</sup> dry soil. The <i>pmoA</i> diversity was represented by seven closely related phylotypes; the most abundant phylotype displayed 97.5% identity to <i>pmoA</i> of <i>Candidatus</i> Methyloaffinis lahnbergensis. Further analysis of prokaryote diversity in this soil did not reveal 16S rRNA gene fragments from well-studied methanotrophs of the order <i>Methylococcales</i> and the family <i>Methylocystaceae</i>. The largest group of reads (~4% of all bacterial 16S rRNA gene fragments) that could potentially belong to methanotrophs was classified as uncultivated <i>Beijerinckiaceae</i> bacteria. These reads displayed 96-100 and 95-98% sequence similarity to 16S rRNA gene of <i>Candidatus</i> Methyloaffinis lahnbergensis and "<i>Methylocapsa gorgona"</i> MG08, respectively, and were represented by eight species-level operational taxonomic units (OTUs), two of which were highly abundant. These identification results characterize subarctic upland soils, which are exposed to atmospheric methane concentrations only, as a unique habitat colonized mostly by USC&#x3b1; methanotrophs.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Belova</LastName><ForeName>Svetlana E</ForeName><Initials>SE</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Winogradsky Institute of Microbiology, Research Center of Biotechnology of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Leninsky Ave. 33/2, Moscow 119071, Russia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Danilova</LastName><ForeName>Olga V</ForeName><Initials>OV</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Winogradsky Institute of Microbiology, Research Center of Biotechnology of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Leninsky Ave. 33/2, Moscow 119071, Russia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ivanova</LastName><ForeName>Anastasia A</ForeName><Initials>AA</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Winogradsky Institute of Microbiology, Research Center of Biotechnology of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Leninsky Ave. 33/2, Moscow 119071, Russia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Merkel</LastName><ForeName>Alexander Y</ForeName><Initials>AY</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Winogradsky Institute of Microbiology, Research Center of Biotechnology of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Leninsky Ave. 33/2, Moscow 119071, Russia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dedysh</LastName><ForeName>Svetlana N</ForeName><Initials>SN</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Winogradsky Institute of Microbiology, Research Center of Biotechnology of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Leninsky Ave. 33/2, Moscow 119071, Russia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2020</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>21</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Switzerland</Country><MedlineTA>Microorganisms</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101625893</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2076-2607</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">USC&#x3b1; group</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">acidic soils</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">atmospheric methane oxidation</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">bacterial diversity</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">forested tundra</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">methanotrophic bacteria</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">pmoA gene</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>The authors declare no conflict of interest.</CoiStatement></MedlineCi